  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Remaining dry and seasonable through Wednesday with \nrain chances returning late week.\n\nFair weather conditions are expected to continue through at least \nWednesday morning as broad 1032-1034 mb high pressure meanders \noffshore. The surface high pressure system will sit off the Delmarva \ncoast today before pushing further out into the western Atlantic \nTuesday and Wednesday. Ridging will remain stationary over the \nregion during this time with widespread subsidence and southerly \nreturn flow boosting temperatures above seasonable norms, especially \nduring the Tuesday and Wednesday timeframe. Moisture advection will \ngradually increase tonight and again Tuesday night yielding the \npotential for patchy fog on both Tuesday and Wednesday mornings. \nOutside of the fog, no hazardous weather is expect until late week. \n\nPassing high clouds will remain prevalent today as a shortwave \ndisturbance passes to the south. This will yield highs in the mid to \nupper 60s for most outside the southern Shenandoah Valley where \ntemperatures will be slightly cooler due to cloud cover. Mostly \nsunny skies return Tuesday with highs in the upper 60s and low 70s. \nMid 70s are expected Wednesday with increasing clouds from the \nsouth and west given an incumbent trough of low pressure/cold \nfront approaching from the Mid-South/Ohio River Valley. \n\nMuch needed rain chances finally return Thursday into Friday as the \nsurface ridge buckles and an incumbent upper level trough pushes \neast from the Great Lakes/Ohio River Valley. At the surface, a \nwave of low pressure and an associated cold front will eject \nfrom the mid- Mississippi River Valley into the central \nAppalachians Thursday and off the northern Delmarva/southern NJ \ncoast Friday into Saturday. As a result, expect increased cloud \ncover Wednesday afternoon and evening with rain chances arriving\nfrom the west late Wednesday night into Thursday morning. \nWidespread showers are expected through Thursday with a \npotential linear feature (i.e embedded t- storms) Thursday \nafternoon and evening. The threat for severe weather appears to \nbe low, but not zero. This will be something to monitor in the \ncoming days given the track of the resultant surface low, \npresentation of the trough, and timing of the cold front as it \npasses through. Additional showers are possible Friday as the \nfront works east and a secondary wave of low pressure pushes \nalong the coast. Rainfall amounts will range between 0.50-1.25\" \nwith locally higher totals along the mountains. Amounts have \ntrended downward in the recent model runs with probabilities for\nan inch of rain areawide sitting less than 25 percent. Even \nwith that said, any rain will be much needed given subtle \ndrought expansion from our recent 2+ week dry spell.",
